repl_remove — implementation plan
==================================

status: done (2026-07-09)

references I1..I5 (plans/repl_list). pure pass-through; the dispatcher
entry exists ("remove" => keys ["repl"]). semantics worth pinning:
removal is RECURSIVE (descendants go too) and pin-guarded.

advertised metadata (the builtin table row)
-------------------------------------------

name          repl_remove
description   "Remove a REPL and all sub-REPLs forked from it. Fails
              if any of them is busy executing an operation, or if
              other REPLs were initialized from this REPL's pin
              (unpin dependents or remove them first). The reply
              names every REPL that was removed."
              (source: mcp_server.py remove :395, enriched with the
              pin/busy guards of ir.ML:690.)
inputSchema   {"type": "object",
               "properties": {"repl": {"type": "string"}},
               "required": ["repl"]}
annotations   {"readOnlyHint": false, "idempotentHint": false,
               "destructiveHint": true, "openWorldHint": false}
              spec refinement [OPEN]: the spec's blanket only separates
              read-only from mutating; repl_remove (and repl_truncate)
              destroy state irrecoverably, so they honestly carry
              destructiveHint true. fold into the spec's "advertised
              tool metadata" bullet together with the readOnlyHint
              refinement already flagged by plans/repl_list.
outputSchema  none.

name/argument mapping across the layers
---------------------------------------

  mcp client edge   tools/call "repl_remove", {"repl": "T"}
  scala builtin row backend.ir("remove", [(repl, T)])
  ml dispatcher     "remove" -> Ir.remove (get "repl")
  engine            ir.ML:690 — collect descendants, refuse if any is
                    Claimed or the pin has dependents; delete all;
                    out "Removed \"T\", \"T2\", ..."

implementation steps
--------------------

1. [this tool] builtin row + handler; errors -> isError.
2. tests; rebuild per rules.

assumptions and how to test them
--------------------------------

T1  removing an unknown repl is a status error ("No REPL"), not ok —
    the tool is not idempotent by design; the model learns the id was
    wrong.
    test [ml unit]: remove "nope" -> error; remove "T" twice ->
    second call errors.
T2  removal is recursive and reports every removed id.
    test [ml unit]: init T, fork T2, fork T3 from T2; remove T ->
    output mentions T, T2, T3; repls empty.
T3  pin dependents block removal.
    test [ml unit]: init A [Main], pin A, init B ["pin@A"];
    remove A -> error "depend on its pin"; remove B; remove A -> ok.
T4  a busy descendant blocks removal (nothing is half-removed).
    test [bridge]: slow step on fork T2, remove T concurrently ->
    error "is busy"; after join, remove T -> ok. (needs real
    concurrency; doubles as an I1 async witness.)
T5  full chain: the e2e create/step/remove loop (shared with
    plans/repl_init T7).

done when
